window.AGENDA_BIOS=Object.assign(window.AGENDA_BIOS||{},{"Ruvimbo Forget":"Ruvimbo is a dedicated biostatistician at Wits Planetary Health Research with a strong foundation in public health and a master's in epidemiology and biostatistics from the University of Witwatersrand. Her work focuses on climate and health research, particularly the impact of heat exposure on maternal health.projects to support evidence-based interventions for climate adaptation, particularly addressing heat-related health impacts. She is passionate about strengthening Africa's response to climate change and committed to helping vulnerable communities in resource-limited settings.","Piyumi Gamage":"A dental surgeon and a postgraduate trainee in Health Informatics at the Postgraduate Institute of Medicine, University of Colombo.","Jeroen Maas":"Jeroen Maas is the Director of Access to Care Technology & Partnerships at Philips and the Core Team Lead at Digital Connected Care Coalition (DCCC), a global private sector coalition, where his work is focused on scaling digital health efforts in low- and middle-income settings through building transformative partnerships.","Snehal Patel":"Snehal Patel is Managing Director of Saena Partners, a Singapore-based venture studio, and works with Longitude Health on technology-enabled care delivery in the United States. He co-founded, led, and successfully exited MyDoc, one of Asia’s earliest insurer-linked telemedicine platforms outside China. Trained in medicine, law, and finance, he brings a multidisciplinary perspective to the practical challenges of commercializing and scaling healthcare innovation across markets. He is a Kauffman Fellow and a life member of the Council on Foreign Relations.","Jinnipa Panyawuthikrai":"Jinnipa Panyawuthikrai is the founder and Managing Director of PharmCare, a Thailand-based online pharmacy platform offering medicine delivery, and professional consultations on medication use and health concerns.","Dr. Sai Praveen Haranath":"Dr. Sai Praveen Haranath is a US board-certified pulmonary and critical care physician. Based in Hyderabad, India, he serves as Chief Physician and Innovation Officer at Apollo Health Axis. His work has helped pioneer scalable, tech-enabled healthcare models that bridge the gap between urban tertiary centers and underserved communities across India and beyond.","Aurel K. Qian":"Cofounder and Chief Medical Officer | SenseCare;  Adjunct Associate Professor | Fudan University\nProf. Qian is a computational neuroscientist and medical AI expert who leads trustworthy and safety-oriented AI initiatives at SenseCare. Formerly CIO of Zhongshan Hospital, she led its AI-powered digital transformation and a pioneering hospital digital twin initiative featured by Harvard Business Review. She is committed to using technology to improve access to quality healthcare, making care more inclusive and efficient. She also serves on the HIMSS APAC Board and leads the AI advisory team.","Dr. Daniel Tom-Abba":"Dr. Daniel Tom-Abba works at the intersection of epidemiology, health systems and applied AI. He co-architected SORMAS, EpidetectAI, the outbreak surveillance platform that achieved a 100% score on the WHO Global Goods Maturity Model and has been deployed across multiple countries. His work spans outbreak intelligence and surveillance, remote patient monitoring, clinical risk prediction, and the design and governance of AI systems in regulated health settings — from research prototype through to national deployment. He holds a PhD in Infectious Disease Epidemiology from Hannover Medical School, an MPH from the University of Roehampton, and a BSc in Computer Science and Statistics, and has published 24 peer-reviewed papers.","Himanshu Dhingra":"Currently working as an Associate Director in the Digital Health team of William J. Clinton Foundation, Himanshu is leading the WJCF team working with National Health Authority (NHA) to drive adoption of Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission (ABDM) among healthcare professionals. Himanshu is a well-rounded professional with twelve years of experience across social sector advisory and business process automation.\n An MBA graduate from IIM Kozhikode, Himanshu has led and worked on several projects across diverse areas such as agriculture, climate change, digital health, health financing, livelihoods, nutrition, social entrepreneurship, and WASH. With an expertise in business planning and modeling for social enterprises, Himanshu also has significant experience in developing high quality toolkits and documenting complex processes to enable sector-wide learning.\n A consistent academic performer and IIM gold medal holder, Himanshu also holds a bachelor’s degree in technology (computer science) and has also worked across all phases of software development to automate complex business processes.","Vishnu Vasudev":"Vishnu Vasudev is a Director at the Clinton Health Access Initiative (CHAI), where he has spent six years leading work across digital health, health financing, and new initiatives, progressing through roles including Deputy Director and Associate Director. Based in New Delhi, his work supports health systems strengthening in India. Before CHAI, he co-founded Ka Partners and served as Director of Programs at The Antara Foundation, and earlier held roles at Dalberg Global Development Advisors and Oliver Wyman Financial Services, bringing a strong grounding in strategy consulting and financial analysis to the social sector. Vishnu holds an MBA in Analytical Finance from the Indian School of Business.","Ridima Kamal":"Dr. Ridima Kamal (BDS, MPH, PhD Pursuing) is a dentist-turned-public health professional with expertise spanning community health, global health operations, and health systems development. She currently serves as Chief Manager of Partnerships & Collaborations at Aarogya Seva and is the Founder of PHPCG – Public Health Professionals Coalition for Global Impact, one of India's fastest-growing communities for aspiring and mid-career public health professionals. Her fieldwork has taken her across underserved regions of Northeast India, from Mizoram to Majuli in Assam, where she worked directly with rural communities. She is also the creator of RISHI, a wellbeing initiative focused on rebuilding lives after emotional adversity. Driven by compassion and a belief that healthcare begins with human connection, Dr. Ridima continues to build platforms that make public health more inclusive, accessible, and community-centered","Dr Andrew Gonani":"Dr Andrew Gonani directs the Quality Management Directorate and the Digital Health Division at Malawi’s Ministry of Health and Sanitation. He will examine how digital transformation can strengthen service quality, safeguard clinical continuity and maintain accountability as health information systems are consolidated.","Dr Alinafe Mbewe":"Dr Alinafe Mbewe is Deputy Director of Malawi’s Digital Health Division. She will discuss government stewardship of digital health, including national governance, partner coordination, standards and the institutional foundations of sustainable digital public infrastructure.","Dr Joseph Wu":"Dr Joseph Wu is a public health and digital health specialist with nearly two decades of experience in Malawi. He holds a PhD in Informatics from the University of Oslo and an MSc in Epidemiology from National Taiwan University. He has worked at Luke International and has lived in Malawi since 2008; he currently serves as Acting Head of Programmes and Head of Research, Innovation, Monitoring and Evaluation. He has supported Malawi’s Ministry of Health and Sanitation in strengthening digital health governance, interoperable health information systems and One Health surveillance since 2008. His work connects government leadership, local technical capacity and implementation research to advance sustainable, nationally owned digital health infrastructure.","Mark Landry":"Mark Landry is a Programme Officer with the World Health Organization. He will moderate a discussion of government leadership, health-system resilience and the changing responsibilities of digital health partners.","Selamawit Teklehaimanot":"Author Biography\n\nSelamawit Meressa Teklehaimanot is a Senior Monitoring, Evaluation, Learning, and Gender Specialist at JSI's Data Use Partnership (DUP 2.0) funded Gates Foundation in Ethiopia, with over 14 years of professional experience in public health, including more than eight years leading monitoring, evaluation, learning, digital health, health information systems, and gender integration initiatives.\n\nShe has extensive expertise in designing and implementing monitoring and evaluation systems, conducting implementation research, strengthening data quality, developing digital data collection and reporting systems, and promoting evidence-based decision-making. Selamawit has contributed to national digital health and health information system strengthening efforts, including the development of Ethiopia's National Strategy for Mainstreaming Gender within Digital Health and Health Information Systems. She has authored peer-reviewed publications and presented her work at international conferences, including the Global Digital Health Forum, the International Conference on Primary Health Care, the Global Health Supply Chain Summit, and the SBCC Summit.","Bhumika Piya":"Bhumika Piya, PhD, is a Senior MEAL Technical Advisor on the Program Strategy and Innovation’s (PSI) Evidence and Learning team at CARE. In this role, she leads the design and delivery of high-quality MEAL across CARE’s Global Health and Women and Girls portfolios. She leverages monitoring and evaluation data to generate insights and evidence to inform program design, learning, and strategic decision-making. Her work is grounded in innovative methodologies, meaningful capacity sharing within CARE and across the sector, and translation of evidence into positive, measurable impact. Bhumika holds a PhD in Sociology from Vanderbilt University and is based in Kathmandu, Nepal.","Joseline Carías - Galeano":"Joseline Carías Galeano is an international consultant, speaker, mentor, and project management expert recognized for her leadership in digital health across the globe. She currently serves as Executive Director of RECAINSA, one of the region’s most influential digital health networks in LAC, leading initiatives that support the development and implementation of national and regional digital health strategies.\n\nShe is also co-creator of innovative programs focused on digital transformation, women’s leadership, and capacity strengthening in health systems. Her work has contributed to advancing governance, interoperability, workforce development, and regional collaboration in digital health ecosystems across low- and middle-income settings.\n\nJoseline has been recognized by the Inter-American Development Bank (IDB) and the Pan American Health Organization (PAHO) as one of the Women Champions in Digital Health in 2021 and 2022, and by the global coalition Transform Health as one of the leading women shaping the future of digital health worldwide in 2025 and 2026.","Anthony Kabarita":"I am an exceptionally qualified statistician and researcher with over 10 years of experience, specializing in developing and implementing comprehensive MEAL (Monitoring, Evaluation, Accountability, and Learning) frameworks.  I am adept in large database management and advanced statistical analysis using STATA, MySQL, R, SPSS, Excel, MS Access, and ERP systems. My expertise includes conducting impact evaluations, baseline and endline studies, needs assessments, and developing insightful case studies. Working with multinational INGOs has sharpened my vast experience in knowledge management, monitoring and evaluation, as well as social and market research. I'm adept at using methodical data analysis and creating cutting-edge tools and models that promote accountability and learning, enabling organizations, corporations, governments,  including INGOs and NGOs, to increase efficiency and responsiveness.","Vivian Korir":"Vivian Korir is a Workforce Transformation, Digital Health, and EdTech leader with over 15 years of experience designing and leading digital health workforce development programmes across 13+ low- and middle-income countries in Sub-Saharan Africa. She is the Founder of FAST Elevate Africa, a Pan-African social enterprise advancing digitally enabled, future-ready health workforces. Vivian is a co-author of the Global Community Health Worker Foundational Digital Literacy Framework (PATH/Digital Square, 2025) and has supported governments and development partners in strengthening digital capability, learning systems, leadership, and workforce performance. Her work focuses on digital literacy, AI readiness, competency-based learning, and health workforce transformation for resilient health systems.","David Akpan":"David joined eHealth Africa in March 2022 as the Deputy Director, Programs and Impact, now the Partnerships and Programs Directorate. He oversees the coordination and administration of all new and ongoing projects/programs portfolios across Africa. He drives the program design, program implementation strategy, and designing research and evaluation methods.\n\nBefore joining eHA, David was the Technical Lead and Deputy Chief of Party for a $21.5 Million USAID-funded Integrated Child Health and Social Service Award (Lot 1) implemented in Akwa Ibom, Cross River, and Bayelsa States. David successfully led the writing of these awards in recent years: USAID/ICHSSA1 (2019: $21.5Million); USAID/ICHSSA4 (2019: $20.5Million); USAID/ACE C4 (2022: $40Million). He also serves as the lead writer for CCCRN on the 2022 CDC Nigeria RFA: Delivering Comprehensive and Sustainable HIV/AIDS Clinical and Community Services to Achieve HIV Epidemic Control in Subnational Units in Nigeria; and a Thematic Lead writer (Orphans and Vulnerable Children & DREAMS-Sexual Prevention programs) for the CDC RFA Strengthening Care and Support Services for Highly Vulnerable Populations, Orphans and Vulnerable Children and their Families, plus HIV Prevention for Youth in the Republic of Cote d'Ivoire under PEPFAR.\n\nDavid holds a Doctor of Philosophy (Ph.D.) in Public Health Project Management from LIGS University, Hawaii. He also holds three Master’s Degree in Public Health from the University of Roehampton, London; Humanitarian Aid and International Cooperation from KALU Institute in Spain; and Project Leadership and Systems Thinking from Cornell University, New York. David has a first degree in Environmental Education from the University of Calabar, Calabar - Nigeria.","Dominic Gulak":"Dominic Gulak is a Certified Project Management Professional (PMP®) and Associate Manager at eHealth Africa, with over eight years of experience in climate adaptation, digital health innovation, and health systems strengthening across Nigeria. Within eHealth Africa’s Climate Adaptation in Health, Food Security and Nutrition (CAHFSN) programme area, he has played a pivotal role in designing and implementing technology-driven solutions that enhance system resilience and improve data-driven decision-making in low-resource settings.\n\nDominic co-led the design and implementation of the Climate Health Vulnerability Assessment Tool (CHAT)—a digital, human-centred adaptation of the WHO checklist used to assess and visualise vulnerabilities of primary healthcare facilities. His contributions span tool design, digital data integration, stakeholder coordination, and real-time analytics that inform state-level adaptation planning. Passionate about leveraging innovation for climate-resilient systems, he continues to advance solutions that bridge the intersection of climate, health, food security and nutrition in Africa.","Edi Jeremiah":"Edi Jeremiah is a public health and digital innovation specialist with extensive experience in strengthening health systems through technology-driven solutions. At eHealth Africa (eHA), he led the development and deployment of the Climate Health Vulnerability Assessment Tool (CHAT) - a pioneering digital adaptation of the WHO checklist that assesses and strengthens the climate resilience of primary healthcare facilities in Nigeria.\n\nHe brings immense project management and capacity development expertise to bear, leading, climate-health resilience, large-scale public health, community-focused interventions leveraging human-centered-design approach across Nigeria. His expertise spans project design and management, monitoring and evaluation, digital health innovations, stakeholder engagement at both national and sub-national levels. He manages a couple of projects (including CHAT and the REACH Regional Dashboard) for eHealth Africa - a leading indigenous organization well known for strengthening health systems through innovative, data-driven digital solutions and person-centered care. He holds a bachelors degree in Sociology and Masters in Health Management.\n\nHis work integrates climate change adaptation, health systems resilience, and data-driven decision-making, advancing national preparedness for climate-sensitive health risks. Jeremiah’s contributions reflect a strong commitment to leveraging digital innovations for evidence-based planning, sustainability, and improved health outcomes in vulnerable communities.","Shalom J Tchokfe Ndoula":"Dr. Shalom J. Tchokfe Ndoula is a Cameroonian public health physician and epidemiologist, and serves as Director of the Expanded Programme on Immunization (EPI) within Cameroon’s Ministry of Public Health. He has played a leading role in advancing immunization and child health in Cameroon, including the country’s historic 2024 launch of the world’s first routine malaria vaccination programme for children.\n\nHis work focuses on strengthening immunization systems, improving vaccine coverage, and protecting children from preventable diseases. In addition to his leadership on malaria vaccination, Dr. Tchokfe Ndoula has contributed to efforts to expand HPV vaccine uptake and strengthen public health systems for routine immunization and campaign delivery.\n\nAs a trained medical doctor with expertise in epidemiology, he brings both clinical and population-health perspectives to national immunization strategy, implementation, and disease prevention.","Dr Rahul Mathew":"Dr. Rahul Mathew Nimmagadda is a development and partnerships leader with over 15 years of experience designing and scaling innovative solutions across health, financial inclusion, livelihoods, climate resilience, and humanitarian response in Asia. He currently serves as Head of Resource Development and Communications at World Vision Bangladesh, where he leads strategic partnerships, resource mobilization, innovation, and external engagement initiatives.\n\nRahul has worked extensively at the intersection of community development, systems strengthening, and sustainable financing, with a particular interest in developing scalable models that improve access to essential services for vulnerable populations. He is currently leading the development of Project ExtraCover, an innovative initiative that integrates digital health services, microinsurance, and nano-savings to strengthen health protection and financial resilience among low-income households in Bangladesh.\n\nHis work focuses on building bridges between communities, capital, and systems to create sustainable pathways for social impact. Rahul has collaborated with governments, donors, multilateral agencies, private sector partners, and financial institutions to design and scale solutions that address complex development challenges.\n\nHe is particularly interested in exploring how digital health, financial inclusion, and innovative financing can be integrated to create sustainable and equitable pathways to health protection for vulnerable populations.","Dorcas Essien":"Dorcas Essien is a Digital Health Manager at Malaria Consortium Nigeria, where she leads the design, implementation, and scale-up of digital solutions supporting large-scale public health interventions. She oversees digital health strategy and the deployment of digital platforms that strengthen programme delivery, workforce management, data use, and decision-making. With over eight years of experience in digital health and health informatics, she specializes in developing and implementing technology-enabled solutions that improve service delivery, strengthen health systems, and support evidence-based decision-making through high-quality data.","Dr. Bezawit Mesfin Hunegnaw":"Bezawit M. Hunegnaw is the Deputy Projects Director at JSI Ethiopia, with over 15 years of experience in clinical and public health practice, programs leadership and research in RMNCH. She comes with expertise in strategic leadership, health systems strengthening and evidence-based practice. At JSI, she leads the design and implementation of interventions that aim to strengthen service delivery and quality of care in primary health care while also leveraging digital solutions and equitable, human-centered approaches. Prior to this, she has led community and facility-based epidemiologic research in RMNCH, evidence-to-policy translation and capacity building in MNCH research and service delivery. Bezawit earned her MD and specialty certificate in Pediatrics and Child Health from Addis Ababa University, School of Medicine and holds a Master’s degree in Public Health from Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health.","Daniel III Garcia":"Field epidemiologist with over 11 years of experience as a Program Manager of Public Health Surveillance from the City Health Office - City Epidemiology, Health Statistics, Disaster, and Response Unit (CHO-CESDRU) of City Government of Antipolo. Skilled in conducting epidemiological investigations, analyzing data, and developing targeted interventions.","Leila Kaza":"Dr. Leila KAZE is a Medical Doctor with 5 years of experience in malaria control, policy design, monitoring, and evaluation within the National Malaria Control Program (NMCP). She earned her Doctor of Medicine from Xi’an Jiaotong University. She is proficient in multiple languages, including French and English, and is skilled in advanced tools like DHIS2, SurveyCTO, and MS Excel. She currently serves as the Head of Malaria Case Management for the NMCP, leading the coordination, planning, and development of national guidelines and strategic plans, including the National Malaria Strategic Plan (2021–2027). Her expertise includes supervision, training health personnel, managing outbreaks, and validating DHIS2 data. A key aspect of her work involves modernizing diagnosis by supporting the use of digital tools like the Malariya Pi app, developed in collaboration with Audere and the NMCP.","Mathias NSABIMANA":"Director of the National Malaria Control Program (NMCP) of Burundi.","MARTIN GITHIOMI":"Martin is software designer and digital health consultant with extensive experience in spearheading digital transformation initiatives within public health programs. He specializes in onboarding cutting-edge technology concepts to sustain patient-centric solutions, with a proven track record of designing and implementing impactful digital tools for tuberculosis and cancer control programs. Martin is also skilled in project management, data management, and software development, with a commitment to improving health outcomes through technology.","Tom White":"Tom White is Zipline's General Manager for Asia-Pacific, responsible for establishing the company's presence and operations across the region. He joined from Archer Aviation, an eVTOL manufacturer, where he led Asia-Pacific. Before that, he spent nine years at Uber in senior roles across Australia, Vietnam, Japan and Korea, including serving as General Manager for both Vietnam and Japan, and as the founding CEO of Uber's joint venture with SK Group in Seoul. His career has also spanned public life; he served as a ministerial adviser in the Western Australian government and stood as a candidate for Australia's House of Representatives at the most recent national election. He holds degrees from the University of Western Australia, the University of Chicago Booth, and the University of Cambridge.","Dorothy Mailosi":"Dorothy Mailosi is a data scientist and research assistant at CODATA and a master's candidate in data science at Deakin University. Her work spans climate and health analytics, FAIR data implementation, health informatics, and research data infrastructure development. She has contributed to mental health data harmonization projects and the implementation of the OMOP Common Data Model for population health research. Dorothy is currently leading the development of a multi-country climate and health data warehouse integrating Copernicus Earth observation and ground sensor data and health surveillance data across Africa. Her interests include environmental health, climate-informed mortality surveillance, geospatial analytics, and open, interoperable digital infrastructures that enable reproducible research and evidence-based policymaking. She is passionate about using data science to address public health challenges and strengthen climate resilience in African communities.","Yusuf Babatunde":"Yusuf Babatunde is a Growth and Partnerships at YLabs. He started as a Youth Leadership Fellow, building scalable solutions with young people that change how governments, foundations, and large-scale implementers serve them.","Joseph Ross":"Joseph (Joey) Ross, MPA, is an Associate Director on the Primary Health Care team at Ariadne Labs, a dual center for health systems innovation at the Harvard School of Public Health and Brigham and Women’s Hospital. Joey has over 16 years experience working in global public health, building partnerships and managing multidisciplinary research and project implementation teams to translate PHC evidence and innovation into policy and practice. He previously worked in health systems analyses and project implementation roles at John Snow, Inc., and in public health research and strategy development at ICAP at Columbia University and the Global Development Incubator. Joey completed his undergraduate studies at Bucknell University and his master of public administration in development practice from Columbia University.","James Kiilu":"Dr. James Kiilu is a medical doctor with a strong interest in global health, community participation, and equitable healthcare systems. As a member of the Kenya Community Advisory Team (KCAT) under the Digital Health and Rights Project, he contributes to national and global discussions on rights-based digital governance and equitable access to healthcare.\n\nJames is passionate about strengthening participatory approaches that bridge policy and lived experience, ensuring that health systems remain responsive to the needs of communities. He has worked extensively with key populations and youth networks to promote inclusion, accountability, and the integration of community perspectives into health policy and programme design. He previously served as a youth advocate at the Centre for the Study of Adolescence and currently serves as Director of Outreach at the Integrated Cancer Research Foundation, leading initiatives on cancer awareness, early screening, and HPV vaccination. Currently, he serves as a medical officer intern at Kiambu Level 5 hospital in Kenya.","Dr. Nikesh Shah":"Dr. Nikesh R. Shah is a physician and health-systems executive with over twenty years across US and Indian healthcare, and Clinical Advisor for AI R&D at Intelehealth. He previously served as CEO of the National Cancer Grid's Koita Centre for Digital Oncology at Tata Memorial Centre, leading digital transformation of cancer care including oncology EMRs, a national tele-oncology platform, and AI and machine-learning innovations. His work spans clinical documentation, care coordination, and clinical data analytics on the provider side and population health analytics on the payer side. He holds an MBBS from the University of Mumbai and a Master of Health Administration from Cornell University.","Jimmy Patrick Alunyo":"Jimmy Patrick Alunyo is a public health researcher, surveillance specialist, and digital health practitioner affiliated with the Uganda Ministry of Health and Busitema University. His work focuses on infectious disease surveillance, mortality surveillance, outbreak preparedness and response, digital health systems strengthening, epidemiology, and health information systems in low-resource settings. He has contributed to several national and regional initiatives aimed at improving disease detection, public health intelligence, and surveillance capacity across Africa.\n\nJimmy has been involved in research and implementation projects addressing mortality surveillance, antimicrobial stewardship, tuberculosis, community-based surveillance, and outbreak response. His recent work includes leading the development of integrated community mortality surveillance workflows within Uganda’s electronic Community Health Information System (eCHIS) to strengthen outbreak detection and public health decision-making. He has also contributed to capacity-building programs in infectious disease epidemiology and field surveillance, supporting efforts to strengthen health security and emergency preparedness in the region.\n\nHis interests include digital health governance, surveillance system integration, interoperability, implementation research, epidemic preparedness, and the use of data-driven approaches to improve population health outcomes in Africa.","Kinanti Khansa Chavarina":"Kinanti Khansa Chavarina is an Associate at the International Unit of the Health Intervention and Technology Assessment Program (HITAP) Foundation in Thailand. Her work focuses on health policy and systems research, such as scoping and systematic reviews, qualitative analysis, and economic evaluation. Her research interests include health policy, health technology assessment, kidney disease, and digital health. Khansa also manages international collaborations, including ongoing work with Indonesia and previous engagement with the Philippines. She holds a Master of Public Health from the National University of Singapore and a Bachelor’s degree in Pharmacy from Universitas Indonesia.","Vipin Joseph":"Vipin Joseph leads the Strategic Information Division of CHAI. He is a public health professional with 15+ years of experience in MEAL systems, project implementation, partnership building, and knowledge management across HIV/AIDS, TB, Community Health, and Health Systems Strengthening. Skilled in leading donor-funded initiatives, fostering learning cultures, and leveraging digital tools for public health impact. Proven ability to coordinate with government, civil society, and international stakeholders (USAID, CDC, Global Fund, World Bank, PEPFAR, CRS) in advancing inclusive and data-driven healthcare strategies.","Manesh Thomas":"Mr. Manesh Thomas leads CHAI’s information and communication technology initiatives. He is responsible for managing IT infrastructure, digital systems, and ensuring smooth technology operations across the organization. With a background in Physics and additional qualifications in computer applications, hardware, and networking, he contributes to CHAI’s mission by driving digital innovation and supporting technology-enabled solutions.","Michael Waithaka":"Michael Waithaka is a Research and Learning Advisor at Jhpiego and an experienced monitoring, evaluation, research, and learning (MERL) specialist with expertise in data analytics, biostatistics, implementation research, and evidence generation. He currently leads research and learning initiatives, providing technical leadership in study design, protocol development, data quality assurance, knowledge management, and dissemination of research findings. Prior to joining Jhpiego, he served as a Senior Data Analyst with the Performance Monitoring for Action (PMA) program and the International Centre for Reproductive Health–Kenya, and as a Senior Statistician at KEMRI-Wellcome Trust. His work spans reproductive health, family planning, population health, and health systems research. Michael is passionate about leveraging artificial intelligence, machine learning, and digital technologies to generate actionable evidence and strengthen health programs and policy.","Wandia Ikua":"Wandia Ikua is a public health systems specialist, digital health researcher, and Monitoring, Evaluation and Learning (MEL) expert with more than 17 years of experience advancing health programs across Africa. She has built a career at the intersection of health systems strengthening, digital health, health security, and evidence-informed decision-making.\n\nWandia has advised governments, donors, and implementing partners on the design of national monitoring and evaluation frameworks, digital health strategies, health information systems, and health security measurement approaches. Her work spans program implementation, policy development, research, and capacity strengthening, with a particular focus on translating data into actionable evidence for improved health outcomes.\n\nAs a PhD candidate at Strathmore Business School, her research explores what drives the sustainable use of digital public health interventions among frontline health workers in low-resource settings. She brings a unique perspective that combines frontline clinical experience, public health practice, and implementation research to inform the design and scale-up of sustainable digital health solutions.","Nokulunga Twala":"Nokulunga Twala is an Associate in the Health Practice at Genesis Analytics, based in Johannesburg, South Africa. With 3+ years of experience in research, data analysis, and monitoring and evaluation, she specialises in translating complex governance frameworks into actionable diagnostic tools for national planning.\n\nNokulunga supported the DHMI implementation across 10 African countries, generating insights on how governance maturity predicts digital health scaling success. Her professional experience spans multi-country health initiatives across 15+ African countries. As Data Visualisation Lead for the South-South Learning Network, she designed interactive dashboards tracking HIV prevention implementation across diverse African contexts, supporting evidence-based resource allocation and policy decision-making. She has also supported rigorous health economic evaluation work with the South African Medical Research Council, developing financial performance dashboards for innovative health financing mechanisms.\n\nHer technical expertise encompasses data science, geospatial analysis, research design, and the development of data-driven governance instruments. She is currently studying for a Master of Science in GIS and Remote Sensing at the University of the Witwatersrand and holds a B.Sc Honours in Urban and Regional Planning. At GDHF, she will present DHMI findings on governance-led digital health transformation across African countries.","Simon Mbae":"Simon Mbae is a seasoned public health professional with over a decade of expertise at the intersection of community health and digital innovation. Driven by a commitment to accelerating Universal Health Coverage (UHC), he specializes in deploying transformative technologies to strengthen primary healthcare systems in Africa.\n\nCurrently serving as the Program Head for East Africa at Medic Afya, Simon leads strategic partnerships with Ministries of Health in Kenya and Uganda. He provides executive oversight for the implementation of the Community Health Toolkit (CHT) - a global digital public good - which has successfully digitized over 180,000 Community Health Workers. His work focuses on building local technical capacity to ensure the long-term usability, institutional ownership, and operational sustainability of national electronic community health information systems (CHIS).\n\nAs the public health sector increasingly embraces digital transformation, Simon champions the use of innovation to leapfrog traditional barriers to healthcare access. He is a dedicated advocate for safe and ethical practices, focused on delivering context-centric and socially acceptable solutions that align diverse stakeholders around a shared purpose.","Jagjit Kaur":"Dr. Jagjit Kaur is a sociologist, a postdoctoral fellow at the Department of Computer Science, Ashoka University, and a project head at the Koita Centre for Digital Health, Ashoka University, where she leads interdisciplinary research at the intersection of sociology, artificial intelligence, and digital health. She currently heads projects at the Koita Centre for Digital Health focused on digital health readiness, electronic health records, AI in healthcare, and the social determinants of digital health in India. Her research examines how sociocultural factors shape the adoption, implementation, and equity of digital health technologies in public health systems. With a background in sociology and education, she brings a critical social science perspective to the design and governance of digital health and AI systems.","Hana Gebeyehu":"Hana Gebeyehu Mandefro is a Digital Transformation Consultant, Business Analyst, and Engagement Manager with over eight years of experience in digital transformation, enterprise architecture, and organizational capability development. She currently serves as Engagement Manager and Business Analyst for Ethiopia's Digital Health Enterprise Architecture Development Project, supporting the Ministry of Health in advancing its national digital health agenda.\n\nHer expertise includes business architecture, capability mapping, competency framework development, digital strategy, and human-centered service design. Hana holds an MSc in IT Management and an MBA, and her research focused on competency frameworks for digital transformation. Her current work focuses on strengthening workforce capabilities, digital competencies, and governance as foundations for sustainable digital health transformation.","Nigussie Mengesha":"Dr. Nigussie Tadesse Mengesha is an Assistant Professor of Information Systems at the Goodman School of Business, Brock University, Canada. His teaching and research focus on digital transformation, artificial intelligence, business analytics, cloud computing, and information systems strategy.\n\nWith over 20 years of experience in academia, consulting, and industry collaboration, he has worked extensively on digital health, digital banking, cloud adoption, and technology-enabled innovation in developing and emerging economies. His research has been published in leading journals, including the Journal of Business Research, Electronic Commerce Research, and Journal of Global Information Management.\n\nDr. Mengesha also provides advisory services in digital transformation, IT governance, cybersecurity, and enterprise systems.","Mercy Kamau":"Mercy Kamau is a Business Analyst at IntelliSOFT Consulting Limited, Kenya, working at the intersection of clinical medicine and digital health. With a background as a general medical practitioner and clinical analyst, she brings a clinician's lens to the design and implementation of context-appropriate digital health solutions for low-resource settings in Africa. At IntelliSOFT, she has been centrally involved in the design and deployment of the Kisumu County Health Information Platform (KCHIP), contributing to stakeholder engagement, workflow analysis, and interoperability implementation across the maternal and newborn health continuum. Her work bridges clinical understanding and systems thinking to translate digital health strategies into operational reality.","JOHN WANYUNGU":"John Wanyungu’s role in Ministry of Health Republic of Kenya is Division of Community Health Services, Moh, Deputy Head (Kenya). He has been the technical lead who has guided the conversation and collaborated with the Division of Digital health, the MOICT and the DHA to ensure that all aspects the implementation are monitored.","Elmer Ongus":"Elmer Ongus is the Monitoring & Evaluation Officer at Safari Doctors, supporting data systems, reporting, learning and evidence use for last-mile health programmes in Kenya. His work focuses on turning outreach, school health, Youth Health Ambassador and One Health data into practical decisions for climate-resilient, community-led care.","Sujay Santra":"Sujay Santra, Founder & CEO of iKure TechSoft, is a distinguished social entrepreneur, an Ashoka Fellow and a member of the National Core Committee on Artificial Intelligence at CII. Sujay has been honored by former President Pranab Mukherjee for his contributions to social impact. Under his leadership, iKure is providing affordable healthcare across rural India and has impacted 34 million lives. Author of Losing Big, Winning Bigger and a sought-after global speaker, he advocates for equitable healthcare on global platforms like MIT, the UN, and UNDP.","Saptak Ghosal":"Public Health and Epidemiology professional with 4+ years of experience in research, monitoring and evaluation (M&E), health program implementation, and data analysis. Proficient in study design, data collection, statistical analysis, program monitoring, stakeholder engagement, team supervision, and grant-funded project management. Strong track record of working in multidisciplinary environments to achieve program and research objectives.","Karen Finnegan":"Karen Finnegan, PhD, MPH, is a public health researcher with more than twenty years of experience conducting research into health systems in countries around the world. She is a Research Scientist at Harvard Medical School and the Director of Science and Impact at Pivot, leading research activities for the NGO, which works to strengthen the public health system in rural Madagascar.","Wubshet Denboba Midekssa":"Wubshet is a seasoned public health professional with about 20 years of experience in Ethiopia’s health sector, currently serving as Project Director at JSI. He holds a Master’s degree in Public Health and brings deep expertise in designing and implementing health information systems, as well as health policy and planning.\n\nBeginning his career as a public health practitioner and manager at a district-level Health Center, Wubshet went on to work with PEPFAR-funded international NGOs for more than a decade. During this time, he played a leading role in developing and institutionalizing Ethiopia’s health management information system (HMIS), transitioning the country from fragmented data systems to a more integrated national framework. His work has spanned capacity building, mentorship, policy development, and system-level strategy.\n\nSince 2019, Wubshet has taken on various technical, supervisory, and leadership roles within JSI, supporting the Ethiopian Ministry of Health’s Information Revolution agenda. His efforts have been critical in advancing a culture of data use, digitization, and health information system governance. Wubshet is known for his strategic vision, close collaboration with government partners, and commitment to strengthening local institutions, including universities and civil society organizations.","Abebaw Gebeyehu Worku":"Dr Abebaw Gebeyehu is a Senior Public Health Professional and associate professor in reproductive health with extensive experience in health systems, research, and academia. Dr. Abebaw holds a Ph.D. from Addis Ababa University, an MPH from the University of Gondar, and a first degree in Public Health from Debub University. Currently he is leading MERL activities at JSI/Data Use Partnership and serve as senior advisor of the MOH- PSR LEO. He\n has previously held key leadership roles, including Head of the Amhara Regional Health Bureau and Director of the Institute of Public Health at the University of Gondar. Throughout his career, which began with health center leadership in North Gondar, he has made significant academic contributions by developing curricula for 17 postgraduate programs, supervising over 55 gradute students ( 15 PhD &amp; over 40 MPH) and publishing over 120 articles in reputable journals.","Saumyadeb Dasgupta":"Saumyadeb Dasgupta manages Dimagi's community Kangaroo Mother Care program across Uganda, Kenya, Nigeria, and India. Working with locally led organizations, the program focuses on improving outcomes for preterm and low-birthweight infants once they leave the health facility, with frontline health workers visiting their households through this fragile period. The financing structure follows a pay-per-completed-case model built on a carefully designed protocol that guarantees a consistent standard of care for every infant while preserving the flexibility to intervene more intensively when needed. The result is a highly scalable, outcome-based approach that is cost-effective and replicable across geographies.","Roshan Koju":"Dr. Roshan Koju is an IT and Digital Transformation Specialist at Nepal's Social Security Fund (SSF), where he leads initiatives in digital health financing, interoperability, and enterprise architecture. He has played a key role in designing and implementing Nepal's national health purchasing platform based on openIMIS, enabling the integration of public and private healthcare providers through HL7 FHIR-compliant interoperability standards. His work focuses on Digital Public Goods, API-driven system integration, health information exchange, claims automation, cybersecurity, and digital governance for large-scale public sector systems. Through these initiatives, he has contributed to strengthening strategic purchasing, improving transparency and efficiency in healthcare financing, and advancing universal health coverage. His professional interests include digital public infrastructure, artificial intelligence, enterprise architecture, cybersecurity, and technology-driven innovation for sustainable social protection and public service delivery.","Keshab Bahadur Dhami":"Network and Security Professional with 6+ years of experience in enterprise IT infrastructure, network administration, cybersecurity, virtualization, and system operations. Experienced in managing and troubleshooting FortiGate firewalls, F5 WAF/LTM, Sophos, VPNs, enterprise switching, VMware/vSphere, SAN/NAS storage, backup systems, and network monitoring.","África Periáñez Santiago":"África Periáñez Santiago\n CoFounder & CEO\n https://www.linkedin.com/in/africaperianez/\n África is an AI scientist and entrepreneur. África held the role of Chief Analytics Officer at Inditex, leading the AI strategy and bolstering one of the world's fastest supply chains.\n Before, África held the role of Chief Analytics Officer at Inditex. Previously, she founded Yokozuna Data, an AI firm based in Tokyo dedicated to nudging and predicting individual video game player behavior. África holds a Ph.D. in Mathematics from the University of Reading and Master's degrees in String Theory and Theoretical Physics from CERN and the Autonomous University of Madrid. She has also served as a Marie Curie EU research fellow at CERN and worked as a scientist at renowned institutions like RIKEN in Japan and the German Weather Service.","Karthika V Menon":"Karthika V Menon is a doctoral researcher in Economics at the Indian Institute of Technology (IIT) Roorkee, specialising in health economics, health financing, and behavioural economics. Her research sits at the intersection of environmental health, household financial protection, and public policy, with a particular focus on how ambient air pollution, insurance coverage, and social determinants interact to shape healthcare expenditure among vulnerable households in India.\n\nKarthika holds a Master of Arts in Economics from the University of Kerala, where she graduated with distinction, and a Bachelor of Arts in Economics from Government College for Women, Trivandrum. She has also completed coursework in Managerial Economics at the Indian Institute of Management, Bengaluru.\n\nHer research experience spans impact evaluation, policy analysis, and large-scale household survey data, having contributed to projects funded by NABARD, the Government of Kerala, and ICSSR. She has previously served as an Assistant Professor of Econometrics at Alphonsa College, Pala, and as a Research Assistant at the Centre for Agroecology and Public Health.\n\nKarthika has presented her work at several national and international forums, including the AHEAD 2025 International Conference at IIT Roorkee, the Monsoon School on Inequality jointly organised by YSI-INET and the Centre for Development Studies, and the TAPMI-MaxPlanck Winter School on Boosting Informed Decisions.\n\nShe is a UGC NET holder and is proficient in a range of statistical and econometric software including R Studio, Stata, SPSS, and NVivo.\n\nHer published work includes a paper in the prestigious journal Social Science and Medicine titled \"Does Integrating Pharmaceutical Coverage into Publicly Financed Health Insurance Reduce Out-of-Pocket Spending? Difference-in-Differences Evidence from Kerala, India\" — reflecting her sustained commitment to generating rigorous causal evidence on health financing policy in the Indian context.","Flavian Otieno":"I am a Data Scientist at the African Population and Health Research Center (APHRC), with a background in Mathematics and Statistics. My research focuses on climate-health modeling, with a focus on climate-sensitive diseases, mental health, maternal and child health, and stroke in relation to climate variability. I am an early-career researcher.","Priyanga Senanayaka":"Dr. Priyanga Senanayaka is a Specialist in Digital Health and Consultant in Health Informatics at the Family Health Bureau, Ministry of Health, Sri Lanka. He previously served as a Digital Health Consultant with the World Health Organization in Sri Lanka and the Maldives, leading the design and implementation of national digital health initiatives.\n\nHe holds an MD in Health Informatics (Gold Medal), an MSc in Biomedical Informatics, MBBS and is a TOGAF® 9 Certified Enterprise Architect, HL7® FHIR® Proficient, and OpenHIE Foundation Level Certified.\n\nHis expertise includes DHIS2, interoperability, digital public infrastructure, health information systems, climate and health, and digital health governance. He has led the development of several national digital health platforms and regularly contributes to capacity building, research, and international digital health collaborations.","Dércio Duvane":"Dércio Duvane is a Senior Manager for Health Information Systems based in Maputo, Mozambique, with expertise in digital health, artificial intelligence (AI), telehealth, health information systems, and data governance. He works at the intersection of technology and public health, leading initiatives that strengthen health systems through innovative digital solutions, improved data use, disease surveillance, and evidence-based decision-making. Dércio has supported the design and implementation of AI-enabled and telehealth interventions, collaborating with governments, development partners, and technical stakeholders to expand access to quality healthcare and accelerate digital transformation across the health sector.","Benjamin Shaibu":"Shaibu Benjamin Ojoka is a Spatial Data Scientist with 7+ years of experience integrating GIS, remote sensing, and AI to drive spatial intelligence across health, climate resilience, and development projects in Sub-Saharan Africa.\n\nShort third-person (LinkedIn/website, ~120 words)\n\nShaibu Benjamin Ojoka is a Spatial Data Scientist with over seven years of experience applying GIS, remote sensing, and AI to solve problems in public health, climate resilience, and development across Sub-Saharan Africa. Currently Senior Coordinator, GIS & Data Analytics at eHealth Africa, he builds geospatial models, AI-driven decision-support tools, and automated data pipelines using Python, Google Earth Engine, and cloud platforms. His work spans disease-outbreak tracking, climate vulnerability assessment, and immunization-coverage mapping across Nigeria, Mali, and Niger. He holds an M.Sc. in Exploration Geophysics (Distinction) and is pursuing a PhD in Coastal Zone Management & Climate Change at the University of Port Harcourt. He is also a published researcher focused on coastal vulnerability and environmental impact modeling.","Sharon Alalouf":"Sharon Alalouf is a Regional Business Development Director driving strategic growth and market development across APAC. Her work spans AI infrastructure, Edge AI, Autonomous Networks, IoT and next-generation connected technologies, translating technology innovation into new markets and business opportunities. She connects customers, partners, governments, and technology ecosystems to build strategic collaborations and accelerate the path from innovation to commercial impact.","Surajit Nundy":"Surajit Nundy is a physician, a neuroscientist, and the founder of Raxa, where he leads the development of privacy-centric, on-device AI architectures and digital health infrastructure. He focuses on edge AI, local-first data systems, and user data sovereignty in healthcare. An MD, PhD, and Harvard MPH trained at Mass General/Harvard Medical School, he is published in journals including BMJ , NEJM and Nature . Surajit returned to India with a commitment to ensuring quality healthcare for all. He worked in rural India and underserved urban communities before founding Raxa, where he now drives People-Led AI in Health for the Global South.","SV Krishnan":"SV Krishnan is a serial social impact entrepreneur and the Co-founder and CEO of HearSight Audio Vision Pvt. Ltd., where he is leading the development of an assistive wearable device that enables persons with visual impairement to navigate independently. Drawing on over 15 years of working closely with blind people, he is building technology shaped around their real, everyday use cases. Krishnan’s work is driven by a commitment to change how India sees disability, not as a limitation but as a different way of experiencing the world. His work focuses on shifting mindsets and opening pathways to dignified livelihoods and independence for persons with disabilities. In 2011, he brought Dialogue in the Dark to India, helping over five lakh people experience inclusion firsthand and enabling employment for 4,000 persons with disabilities. In 2022, he launched DesiNation, India’s first inclusion-focused e-learning platform, which has reached over one million employees.","Ittichai Phoomsirivilai":"Ittichai Phoomsirivilai is the CEO and Founder of GreenIO, a technology company specializing in Edge AI, AI on Device, IoT, and intelligent digital solutions for healthcare, healthy aging, sustainability, and smart communities. With a background in software engineering and a passion for technology for social good, Ittichai leads the development of privacy-preserving AI solutions that operate directly on local devices, enabling real-time intelligence while reducing cloud dependence. Through collaborations with industry, academia, healthcare providers, and global technology partners, he focuses on applying AI to improve everyday wellbeing, support caregivers, empower older adults, and enhance access to digital health services. His work bridges innovation and practical impact, demonstrating how AI can create meaningful benefits for individuals, families, and communities.","Amanda Bendor":"Amanda Bendor is a global health professional with over 20 years of experience advancing digital health, health systems strengthening, and workforce development in low-resource settings. At the World Bank Group, she supports a range of digital health initiatives including advising countries on the development of digital health architecture, developing resources at the intersection of digital health and NCDs/mental health, and elevating gender equity and leadership in digital health. Amanda has also worked with GIZ, Sonder Collective, and Johns Hopkins University to advance global health initiatives. Previously, she served as Deputy Director of Partnerships at PATH’s Digital Square, where she led global engagement efforts and helped mobilize over $100 million in digital health investment. With a strong focus on building strategic partnerships, Amanda connects technical expertise with country priorities and policy goals to support sustainable health impact. She holds a Master of Public Health from the University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill and is currently a WomenLift Health Fellow.","Chaminda Weerabaddana":"Dr. Chaminda Weerabaddana is a passionate digital health practitioner and a specialist in Health Informatics at Sri Lanka’s Ministry of Health. He leads Sri Lanka’s National Electronic Health Record (NEHR) initiative and the country’s adoption of HL7 FHIR-based interoperability, advancing connected care through standards-based health information exchange. Having served in the Ministry since 2003, he combines clinical experience in general medicine, surgery and neurointensive care with expertise in health informatics, enterprise architecture, Picture Archiving and Communication Systems (PACS), and project management. He has also supported digital health blueprint development in the regional countries Maldives, Nepal and Timor-Leste. His work focuses on building a connected, patient-centred digital health ecosystem for Sri Lanka.","Fred Hersch":"Dr. Fred Hersch is a Senior Product Manager at the Google DeepMind Impact Accelerator, where he leads AI for Social Impact initiatives in global public health. A self-described \"Global Health Technologist,\" he is a medical doctor, social entrepreneur, and lapsed software engineer working at the intersection of health and technology since the early days of web-based telemedicine. Previously the Product Lead for Open Health Stack (OHS) at Google Research, he led its transition to the Linux Foundation - establishing a neutral, community-owned home for open-source building blocks that help developers everywhere create standards-based, AI-ready health solutions. He continues to serve the OHS Software Foundation as a Google Governing Board Member and \"Acting\" Executive Director. Across his work, Fred is dedicated to catalyzing sustainable approaches to local innovation that advance safe, effective, AI-powered digital health.","Isha Dagar":"Isha Dagar is a Project Scientist at the Department of Management Studies, Indian Institute of Technology Delhi, working at the intersection of public health systems, digital health, programme implementation, and data-driven decision support. She holds a Master of Public Health degree and has experience in non-communicable disease research, tobacco cessation, cancer prevention, health systems strengthening, and public health programme monitoring. At IIT Delhi, she has contributed to multidisciplinary projects involving digital health, patient pathway analytics, programme evaluation, stakeholder engagement, and evidence-to-policy translation. Her current work includes supporting IIT Delhi’s collaboration with CAPED on strengthening cancer patient pathway tracking by leveraging existing NP-NCD Portal data to improve continuity of care, referral visibility, and action-oriented programme decision-making. Her research interests include digital public health infrastructure, NCD programme strengthening, cancer screening pathways, implementation research, and equitable use of routine health data for better public health outcomes.","Sanjay Dhir":"Prof. Sanjay Dhir is Professor of Strategic Management and Area Chair at the Department of Management Studies, Indian Institute of Technology Delhi. His research and consulting work spans strategic innovation, public health systems, digital health, implementation research, healthcare equity, programme evaluation, and evidence-to-policy translation. He has led and contributed to several multidisciplinary projects with national and international partners, including work related to health systems strengthening, COVID-19 vaccination rollout, oxygen infrastructure, digital health, and public health programme implementation. At IIT Delhi, he guides research that bridges management science, public health, and data-driven decision-making. His current work includes strengthening cancer patient pathway tracking by leveraging existing NP-NCD Portal data to improve continuity of care, referral visibility, and programme-level decision support.","Jacob Mutale":"Jacob Mutale is Health Informatics Manager in the Strategic Information Department at the Centre for Infectious Disease Research in Zambia (CIDRZ). He has more than thirteen years of experience in health information systems, data management and software development. His work covers electronic health records (SmartCare), DHIS2, interoperability, AI-assisted digitisation of paper health registers, and data governance under Zambia’s Data Protection Act.\n\nHe led the development of Zambia’s national Digital Health Data Governance Framework and its health informatics competency framework. Jacob is a PhD candidate in Medical Informatics and Telehealth at the University of KwaZulu-Natal. His research looks at how mobile health can improve HIV care outcomes for young people in Zambia. He co-supervises Africa CDC public health informatics fellows and reviews ratings for the National Research Foundation of South Africa.","Vanessa D'Souza":"Vanessa D’Souza has served as CEO of SNEHA since March 2013, following a volunteering stint with the organisation. Prior to that, she worked with Citibank India in various positions, her last role being: Director, Citigroup Private Bank. During her tenure with Citibank she worked across various divisions including Non-Resident Indian Business, Corporate Bank, Project Re-engineering and eventually with the Private Bank. She won the coveted Citicorp Chairman’s Service Excellence Award in 1989 for exemplary performance. She holds a Bachelors (Honours) degree in Economics and a postgraduate degree in Management with a specialization in Marketing. Vanessa pursued the Management Executive Development Programme at Stanford University, USA, sponsored by Citibank. She also holds a postgraduate Diploma in Public Health Nutrition. She was a Dasra Social Impact Fellow in 2015 and attended their Leadership Programme for non-profit executives. Vanessa has been a recipient of the Mother Teresa Social Leadership Scholarship, to attend the Strategic Perspectives in Non-Profit Management Programme at Harvard Business School in 2017.","Danya Siddiqi":"Ms. Danya Arif Siddiqi is a Director at Grassroots and an Assistant Professor at the London School of Hygiene & Tropical Medicine. She is a development practitioner specializing in global health. Danya earned her undergraduate degree in Economics from Pakistan and was awarded the prestigious Commonwealth Scholarship to pursue her graduate degree in Development Management at The London School of Economics and Political Science. With over a decade of experience, Danya specializes in program implementation, digital health, impact evaluation, and research within the public health sector. Her expertise encompasses maternal and child health, vaccines, digital health solutions, big data and geospatial analysis for evidence-based decisions and behavioural interventions including economic incentives.\n\nDanya has a proven track record of leading large-scale vaccine research and service delivery programs, addressing both supply- and demand-side challenges to improve vaccine access, coverage, and equity. She has provided technical assistance to governments and collaborated with prominent multilateral organizations, including the WHO, UNICEF, Gavi and the Gates Foundation, contributing to evidence generation, policy formulation, and large-scale program implementation."});
